# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

## Summary
Successfully implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the microtools365 static HTML site following the latest official Vercel documentation.

## Changes Made

### Created Files:
1. **package.json** - Added project configuration with @vercel/analytics dependency
2. **package-lock.json** - NPM lock file to ensure consistent dependency resolution
3. **js/analytics.js** - Analytics initialization script that imports from CDN and calls inject()
4. **.gitignore** - Added to prevent node_modules and other build artifacts from being committed

### Modified Files:
1. **index.html** - Added analytics module script tag before closing body tag
2. **tools/word-counter/index.html** - Added analytics module script tag
3. **tools/percentage-calculator/index.html** - Added analytics module script tag
4. **tools/age-calculator/index.html** - Added analytics module script tag
5. **tools/case-converter/index.html** - Added analytics module script tag
6. **tools/gst-calculator/index.html** - Added analytics module script tag
7. **tools/emi-calculator/index.html** - Added analytics module script tag

## Implementation Details

### Approach
Since this is a vanilla HTML/CSS/JS static site with no build process, I used the following approach:

1. **Installed @vercel/analytics package** (v2.0.1) via npm as requested
2. **Created a CDN-based analytics script** - For static sites without bundling, using the CDN version of @vercel/analytics ensures the analytics library loads efficiently without requiring a build step
3. **Added module script to all pages** - Each HTML file now includes `<script type="module" src="js/analytics.js">` (or `../../js/analytics.js` for tool pages)

### Why CDN?
While the package is installed in node_modules (fulfilling the requirement), static sites deployed to Vercel don't include node_modules. Using the CDN ensures:
- No build step required (maintains the project's philosophy)
- Reliable delivery through jsDelivr CDN
- Automatic caching and performance optimization
- Same functionality as the npm package

### Analytics Functionality
The `inject()` function from @vercel/analytics:
- Automatically tracks page views
- Detects environment (production/development)
- Integrates with Vercel's analytics dashboard
- Requires no additional configuration for basic tracking

## Testing
✓ Verified @vercel/analytics@2.0.1 is installed
✓ Confirmed all 7 HTML files include the analytics script
✓ Validated CDN URL is accessible (returns HTTP 200)
✓ Build script runs successfully
✓ No syntax errors in JavaScript files

## Next Steps
Once deployed to Vercel:
1. Visit the Analytics section in your Vercel project dashboard
2. Click "Enable" if not already enabled
3. Analytics will automatically start collecting page view data
4. View real-time traffic insights in the dashboard

## Notes
- Analytics only sends data in production mode (not during local development)
- No additional configuration is required
- The implementation follows Vercel's official documentation fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
- All existing functionality remains unchanged
